Thinking: We need to answer user question: "How do I load a .pmtiles file as a source in MapLibre GL JS?" Provide step-by-step instructions, code examples, mention installing pmtiles library, registering protocol, using url, handling CORS, raster vs vector, example with React, etc. Use skill content. Provide final answer.

Below is a complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready recipe for loading a **.pmtiles** archive (vector, raster or raster‑DEM) into a MapLibre GL JS map.

---

## 1️⃣ Install the PMTiles protocol library

MapLibre itself does not understand the `pmtiles://` scheme, so you need the tiny helper library that turns a URL into a TileJSON and serves the individual byte‑range requests.

```bash
# Using npm (or yarn/pnpm)
npm install pmtiles
# If you only need the CLI you can install it globally, but for the browser you need the JS package.
```

---

## 2️⃣ Register the protocol **once** before any map is created

```js
// src/pmtiles-setup.js  (or any file that runs at app start)
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import * as maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// Create a protocol instance – it holds an internal LRU cache of fetched ranges
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// Tell MapLibre to use it for the `pmtiles` scheme
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);

// (optional) expose a cleanup function if you ever need to remove it
export const removePmtilesProtocol = () => maplibregl.removeProtocol('pmtiles');
```

> **Why do this once?**  
> The protocol registers a fetch‑handler on the global `XMLHttpRequest`/`fetch` level. Registering it repeatedly (e.g. inside a React component’s `useEffect`) creates duplicate handlers and can leak memory.

---

## 3️⃣ Add a source that points at the archive

### 3.1 Vector tiles (the most common case)

```js
// map initialization (anywhere after the protocol registration)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',               // id of the <div>
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      // One source that points at the whole .pmtiles file
      myPmtiles: {
        type: 'vector',
        // The `pmtiles://` prefix tells MapLibre to use the protocol we added.
        // The URL after the prefix can be any HTTP(S) location that supports
        // Range requests (S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages, CDN, etc.).
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/tiles/world.pmtiles'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      // Example: draw the "water" layer that lives inside the archive
      {
        id: 'water-fill',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'water',          // exact layer name inside the PMTiles file
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      },

      // Example: draw roads
      {
        id: 'road-line',
        type: 'line',
        source: 'myPmtiles',
        'source-layer': 'road',
        paint: {
          'line-color': '#777',
          'line-width': 1
        }
      }

      // …add as many layers as you need, each with its own `source-layer`
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

**Key points**

| What | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| `type: 'vector'` | Tells MapLibre the archive contains vector tiles. |
| `url: 'pmtiles://…'` | The protocol reads the PMTiles header, extracts `minzoom`, `maxzoom`, bounds, and returns a proper TileJSON to MapLibre. **Never use `tiles: [...]`** for PMTiles – you’ll lose the header‑derived zoom range. |
| `'source-layer': '<layerName>'` | The name of a layer inside the archive (e.g. `water`, `road`, `building`). You can discover these names with `pmtiles show <file>` or by looking at the source’s documentation. |
| One source, many layers | A single .pmtiles file can hold dozens of layers; you only need one source entry. |

---

### 3.2 Raster imagery (PNG/JPEG tiles)

```js
{
  id: 'satellite',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'myPmtiles',
  paint: { 'raster-opacity': 0.9 }
}
```

The source definition stays the same (`type: 'raster'` instead of `'vector'`). The archive must contain raster tiles (e.g. a satellite image mosaic). The rest of the style works exactly like any other raster source.

---

### 3.3 Raster‑DEM (terrain) – for hill‑shade / 3‑D terrain

```js
// Add the DEM source
map.addSource('terrain', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'pmtiles://https://my-cdn.example.com/elevation/terrain.pmtiles',
  // Most DEM archives use the Terrarium encoding, but Mapbox‑compatible ones use "mapbox".
  encoding: 'terrarium'   // or "mapbox"
});

// Enable 3‑D terrain in the style
map.setTerrain({ source: 'terrain', exaggeration: 1.5 });
```

Now you can add a `hillshade` layer or enable `terrain` in the style JSON.

---

## 4️⃣ Hosting requirements (so the browser can actually fetch the file)

| Requirement | How to satisfy |
|-------------|----------------|
| **HTTP Range support** | Any static host that returns `Accept-Ranges: bytes` (S3, Cloudflare R2, GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, etc.). |
| **CORS** | The host must send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (or your domain) and `Access-Control-Allow-Headers: Range`. Most CDNs add this automatically for public buckets; otherwise set it in the bucket/CORS config. |
| **Cache headers** | Because the file never changes, set a long `Cache‑Control` (e.g. `public, max-age=31536000`). CDNs will cache the range responses, giving you sub‑second tile loads worldwide. |
| **File size limits** | GitHub Pages caps at 100 MiB per file; S3/R2 have no practical limit. If your dataset is > 5 GiB you may want to split it into regional PMTiles files and use multiple sources. |

