## TL;DR  

MapLibre GL JS v4 changed the **protocol API**:

| v3 (`addProtocol`) | v4 (`addProtocol`) |
|--------------------|--------------------|
| `addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => { … callback(null, data) })` | `addProtocol('my', (params, abortController) => { … return dataOrPromise })` |
| The callback was **mandatory** and had to be called exactly once. | The handler **must return** either a **`Promise`** or a **plain value** (`ArrayBuffer`, `Response`, `string`, …). |
| No built‑in abort support. | The second argument is an **`AbortController`** you can use to cancel a fetch. |

Because of that, any code that still uses the old `(params, cb)` signature simply never resolves the request, so the map shows “no tiles”.

Below is a step‑by‑step migration guide, a minimal working example, and a quick cheat‑sheet for the most common patterns (including the **PMTiles** library, which already ships a v4‑compatible protocol handler).

---

## 1️⃣ What changed in the API

```js
// v3
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, callback) => {
  // … do something async …
  callback(null, arrayBuffer)   // success
  // or
  callback(error)               // failure
});
```

```js
// v4
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, abortController) => {
  // You can:
  //   • return a plain value (synchronous)
  //   • return a Promise that resolves to the value
  //   • return a Response object (fetch‑like)
  //   • throw / reject to signal an error
  // The second argument lets you abort long‑running work.
});
```

* **No more callback** – the function’s *return value* is the result.
* **Abort support** – if you start a `fetch`, pass `abortController.signal` to it.
* **Return types** – `ArrayBuffer`, `Uint8Array`, `Blob`, `Response`, `string`, or a `Promise` that resolves to any of those.

> **Why?**  
> Returning a value (or a promise) lets MapLibre treat the handler like any other async source (e.g. `fetch`). It also makes it possible to use `await`/`async` syntax and to integrate with modern libraries that already return promises.

---

## 2️⃣ Simple “fetch‑and‑return” migration

If your old handler was just a thin wrapper around `fetch`, the migration is trivial:

```js
// BEFORE (v3)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, cb) => {
  const url = params.url;               // e.g. "my://tiles/12/3456/7890.pbf"
  fetch(url)
    .then(r => r.arrayBuffer())
    .then(buf => cb(null, buf))
    .catch(err => cb(err));
});
```

```js
// AFTER (v4)
maplibregl.addProtocol('my', (params, abort) => {
  const url = params.url;               // same shape as before
  // Pass the abort signal so the request can be cancelled if the map
  // no longer needs the tile (e.g. fast pan/zoom).
  return fetch(url, { signal: abort.signal })
    .then(r => {
      if (!r.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${r.status}`);
      return r.arrayBuffer();           // <-- this is the value MapLibre expects
    });
});
```

That’s it – the map will now load tiles again.

---

## 3️⃣ When you need **custom logic** (e.g. in‑memory cache, DB lookup, or a PMTiles file)

### 3.1 Return a plain value (synchronous)

If you already have the tile data in memory (e.g. a `Map<string, Uint8Array>`), just return it:

```js
const tileCache = new Map(); // key = `${z}/${x}/${y}` → Uint8Array

maplibregl.addProtocol('mem', (params) => {
  const key = `${params.z}/${params.x}/${params.y}`;
  const data = tileCache.get(key);
  if (!data) throw new Error('Tile not found');
  // Returning a Uint8Array works directly.
  return data;
});
```

### 3.2 Return a `Promise` that resolves later (async DB, WebWorker, etc.)

```js
maplibregl.addProtocol('db', async (params, abort) => {
  // Example: async function that reads from IndexedDB or a remote API
  const tile = await readTileFromIndexedDB(params.z, params.x, params.y, abort);
  if (!tile) throw new Error('Tile missing');
  return tile; // Uint8Array / ArrayBuffer
});
```

**Abort handling** – if your async routine can be cancelled, listen to `abort.signal`:

```js
function readTileFromIndexedDB(z, x, y, abort) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const request = indexedDB.open('tiles');
    abort.signal.addEventListener('abort', () => {
      request.abort?.(); // optional, depends on API
      reject(new DOMException('Aborted', 'AbortError'));
    });
    // … normal IndexedDB logic that eventually calls resolve(tile) …
  });
}
```

### 3.3 Return a `Response` object (when you already have a `fetch`‑like response)

```js
maplibregl.addProtocol('proxy', (params, abort) => {
  const upstream = `https://my-proxy.com/${params.z}/${params.x}/${params.y}.pbf`;
  return fetch(upstream, { signal: abort.signal });
});
```

MapLibre will read the body of the `Response` automatically (it expects binary data for vector tiles).

---

## 4️⃣ Using the **PMTiles** library (the most common “single‑file” use‑case)

The **official `pmtiles` npm package** already ships a v4‑compatible protocol handler, so you don’t have to write any of the above yourself.

```bash
npm install pmtiles
```

```js
import * as pmtiles from 'pmtiles';
import maplibregl from 'maplibre-gl';

// 1️⃣ Create the protocol instance (it internally caches the file)
const pmtilesProtocol = new pmtiles.Protocol();

// 2️⃣ Register it under the scheme you want to use.
//    The library works with both “pmtiles://…” and plain HTTPS URLs.
maplibregl.addProtocol('pmtiles', pmtilesProtocol.tile);

// 3️⃣ Use it in a style (notice the `url:` – not `tiles:`)
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: {
    version: 8,
    sources: {
      world: {
        type: 'vector',
        // The protocol handler will fetch the .pmtiles file via range requests.
        url: 'pmtiles://https://my-bucket.s3.amazonaws.com/world.pmtiles'
      }
    },
    layers: [
      {
        id: 'water',
        type: 'fill',
        source: 'world',
        'source-layer': 'water',
        paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
      }
      // …more layers…
    ]
  },
  center: [0, 0],
  zoom: 2
});
```

**Why this works in v4:**  
`pmtilesProtocol.tile` returns a **Promise** that resolves to an `ArrayBuffer` (the raw tile data). The library also respects the `AbortController` passed by MapLibre, so long‑running range requests are cancelled when the map no longer needs the tile.

### 4.1 If you need a *custom* protocol **on top of** PMTiles

Sometimes you want to combine a PMTiles file with extra logic (e.g. a fallback to a remote server). You can wrap the PMTiles handler:

```js
const fallback = (params, abort) => fetch(params.url, { signal: abort.signal });

maplibregl.addProtocol('mytiles', async (params, abort) => {
  try {
    // Try the PMTiles archive first
    return await pmtilesProtocol.tile(params, abort);
  } catch (e) {
    // If the tile is missing, fall back to a remote source
    console.warn('PMTiles miss, falling back →', params.url);
    return fallback(params, abort);
  }
});
```

